8. JSP
JSP (JavaServer Pages) is a server‑side technology that lets developers embed Java code into HTML to create dynamic web pages,
and it runs on a Java‑enabled web server such as Apache Tomcat.

9. Difference between the Serlvet and JSP
Servlets are Java classes that run on a web server to handle client requests, process business logic, and generate dynamic responses.
They are ideal for tasks like form processing, database interaction, and controlling application flow. For example,
a servlet can validate login credentials and determine whether a user is authorized to access a dashboard.

JSP (JavaServer Pages) is a server-side technology that allows embedding Java code directly into HTML pages. JSP is primarily used for creating dynamic web pages
and managing the presentation layer. For instance, after a successful login, a JSP page can display a personalized dashboard with user details

10. Key Differences:
Feature											Servlet																JSP
Purpose					Handles business logic, request processing, and control flow				Focuses on presentation and UI generation
Language				Pure Java																	HTML with embedded Java code or JSP tags
Ease of Use				Harder to write HTML; requires out.println statements						Easier to design UI; HTML is straightforward with embedded Java
Role in MVC				Controller																	View
Request Handling		Can handle all protocol requests											Primarily handles HTTP requests
Performance	Faster; 	directly executed as Java class												Slightly slower; compiled into a servlet at runtime
Logic Separation		Business and presentation logic often combined								Business logic separated using JavaBeans or custom tags
Compilation				Precompiled Java class														Compiled into a servlet by the JSP engine at runtime

11. MVC
MVC is a software architectural pattern that separates an application into three components — Model, View, and Controller
to organize code and simplify user‑interface development.

(i) What MVC Means
Model: Manages data, business logic, and rules.
View: Displays data to the user (UI).
Controller: Handles user input, updates the model, and selects the view.

(ii) Why MVC Is Used
Clear separation of concerns
Easier maintenance and scalability
Supports test‑driven development
Common in web frameworks and GUI applications

(iii) History
Created by Trygve Reenskaug in the late 1970s at Xerox PARC for Smalltalk‑80.

(iv) Examples of MVC Frameworks
ASP.NET MVC 
Ruby on Rails
Django (MTV variant)
Spring MVC
